etween vertical distances and path lengths.\n\n**Testing Boundary Logic**\n\nI'm re-evaluating the movement pattern at grid boundaries. It appears that after moving down one step, the subsequent \"down one, left two\" movement can be truncated if it hits the left wall, leading to the next downward step instead. I'm verifying this hypothesis with the provided coordinates.\n\n**Observing Off-Grid Stops**\n\nI'm examining why paths terminate abruptly. It seems that when a \"down one, left two\" movement would send coordinates below zero horizontally, the entire path segment, including the final \"down one\" step, is omitted. I'm confirming this hypothesis.\n\n**Confirming Path Endings**\n\nI've discovered the critical rule: paths terminate immediately if the next step would lead outside the grid boundaries. This perfectly explains all previously observed stopping points across various trains. I'm now applying this logic to new inputs to verify its consistent application.\n\n**Validating End Condition**\n\nI'm testing the hypothesis that paths cease if any subsequent coordinate falls outside the grid. This rule successfully explains all previous path terminations and is being rigorously validated against current inputs.\n\n",
